        "submission": {
          "verdict": "malicious",
          "reasoning": "I'm treating this as malicious: someone loaded a rootkit kernel module on app-01 as root and then set it up to load again. Sequence: the 'ubuntu' account, working in /home/ubuntu/rootkit_test, tried 'modprobe ./rootkit' and 'insmod rootkit.ko' without root. It then ran 'sudo insmod rootkit.ko', which is the alert's trigger process (proc-8fcd04b9b8, running /bin/kmod with parent sudo proc-f571e92c85). That process ran rootkit.ko (file-65389c5c00). Next the account checked with lsmod, ran 'sudo depmod -a' and unloaded the module with 'rmmod rootkit'. It then made a rootkit directory as root, copied rootkit.ko into /lib/modules/5.4.0-1060-aws/kernel/drivers/rootkit/ (file-fde8fa071c) and loaded it again with 'modprobe rootkit'. Copying a module named 'rootkit' into the system module tree and loading it with modprobe looks like an attempt to install it for good. The folder name 'rootkit_test' could mean someone was testing, but nothing I checked shows it was an approved test, so I'm handling it as a real compromise.",
